  "prediction": "The Small Business Administration's (SBA) 7(a) loan program provides financing to small businesses through commercial lenders, with the SBA guaranteeing a portion of the loan in case of default. The program aims to serve creditworthy small businesses that cannot obtain credit from conventional lenders at reasonable terms. However, a review of the program found that lenders often fail to document compliance with the credit elsewhere requirement, which is a critical aspect of the program.\n\nThe credit elsewhere requirement ensures that borrowers cannot obtain credit from non-federal sources at reasonable terms. Lenders are required to document the credit elsewhere determination in the loan file, but a review of on-site reviews found that 40% of reviews identified compliance issues with the credit elsewhere documentation requirement. The most common issues were related to lenders' lack of proper internal controls and procedures, as well as lack of awareness of the credit elsewhere documentation requirement.\n\nThe SBA relies on lenders with delegated authority to make credit elsewhere determinations, but the agency does not require its reviewers to document their assessment of lenders' policies and procedures for complying with the credit elsewhere requirement. This hinders the SBA's ability to take informed and effective actions to improve lender compliance with the requirement.\n\nThe report makes three recommendations to the SBA: (1) require reviewers to consistently document their assessments of a lender's policies and practices, (2) use on-site and off-site reviews to routinely collect information on lenders' use of credit elsewhere criteria, and (3) analyze information on lenders' use of credit elsewhere criteria to identify lenders that may be at greater risk of noncompliance.\n\nThe report also found that the SBA's 7(a) loan program serves a diverse range of borrowers, including new businesses, women-owned businesses, and businesses in economically distressed areas. However, the program's creditworthiness varied widely among borrowers, with some borrowers having much higher credit scores than others. The report also found that lenders with larger numbers of SBA loans tended to have slightly more creditworthy borrowers.\n\nOverall, the report highlights the importance of ensuring that lenders comply with the credit elsewhere requirement and that the SBA has adequate oversight and monitoring mechanisms in place to ensure that the program is serving its intended purpose.",
